Z||000000||hiwil88formula||10-29-2001||09:01 PM||||I have an 88 formula.  I just replaced all the vacuum lines on it they were old and cracked.  I also replaced the thermostat and fan switch to low temp ones.  Before all of this my idle was at 900-1000 rpm now it is at 1100-1200 rpm.  Does this sound right to anyone?  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||samoht||10-29-2001||10:08 PM||||i am not for sure.. I have an 85GT .. but I guess it is possible that the cool water could make it idle higher and get a better idle from better vaccum<P>85gt3.2||||reg||1
Z||000002||RossT||10-30-2001||12:08 AM||||You could still have a vacuum leak somewhere.<P>Double check your egr tube.  I used a dental mirror and found a hair line crack.  Sometimes my formula would idle around 1,000 and other times it would idle around 1200-1500. I just replaced the tube today and the idle came back down to aroun 1,000.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||hiwil88formula||10-30-2001||05:29 PM||||Do the EGR lines crack?  I thought it was metal?  I'll try that next. Thanks!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||johnt671||10-30-2001||05:51 PM||||May take your ECM a while to get use to the changes.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Voytek||10-30-2001||06:33 PM||||Yeah, EGR lines do crack and usually right at the intake plenum.  One way of checking them w/o actual removal is to get some carb spray.  When the car is running, place the little straw that comes with the carb cleaner, right at the tube end (intake plenum).  Spray a bit and see if your idle changes.  If it does, you've got a leak.<P>The EGR leak can be hard to detect.  Ultimately you may have to remove it.<P>Other than that, your idle should be at or below 1000 rpm.  You may have a vacuum leak somewhere else.<P>Johnt671 may be right - if you unhooked the battery, your ECM takes a bit to collect all the data, which may contribute to odd idle.<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnF||10-30-2001||07:05 PM||||You probably have a vaccuum leak!  <P>However, I don't mean to throw cold water on the money, but...<P>I think changing the fan temp and thermostat setting on an '88 is confusing the ECM. First, the fan comes on any time the air conditioner is turned on.  You shouldn't need it any other time.  If you do, changing the ON temp will not help that cooling problem - fix the cooling problem!   Second, the ECM cannot be reprogrammed for these changes externally or internally.  The ECM is set for factory temps as if there were no changes and adjusts the engine to run with a fuel/air mix accordingly.<P>Do some research and I think you will agree.  These two items are worthless at best and are trying to confuse an unchangable ECM at worst!  Get'em out of there.<P>'88s are funny that way!<P>Now, I'll listen to the flames!||||reg||1
